{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792596438,"nonce":"+gnEibUv3McYMwXf9uKMb4SqhFM=","title":"Compiler: retain list element storage through returned header aliases","message":"The interpreter's release-only body for a list passed through an identity function can free the buffer immediately after loading its pointer from the returned header, before the following element GEP/load. The derived-value analysis follows a call's returned alias but does not add that alias to the set of list-header addresses, so loading its element pointer stops the lifetime chain. A phi carrying a header has the same missing header propagation.\n\nReproducer found while exercising unoptimized split-HIR fallback: export function keep\u003cT\u003e(x:T):T{return x;} and score(n:number){const xs=[n,n+2];const ys=keep(xs);ys[1]+=3;return xs[0]+ys[1];}. First interpreter call with n=-4 returns freed-memory bits instead of -3. Full-module inlining masks the issue. Propagate list header identity through proven returned aliases and header phis, preserving the distinction between a header alias and an arbitrary pointer field. Pin the lifetime rule directly in drop_insert tests and verify execution across interpreter/Cranelift/LLVM.\n\nDiscovered while working on 48b697dc20d1db83e3cf17d097527aa61f7a9fb53f291b04e30c6f866512052c.","files":null}]}